
<% content_for :title, "Are You the Fucking Oracle? | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="oracle-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="oracle-hero">

    <picture class="oracle-background" aria-hidden="true">
      <source
        media="(min-width: 768px)"
        srcset="<%= asset_path('toolbox_are_you_the_fucking_oracle_desktop.png') %>"
      >
      <%= image_tag "toolbox_are_you_the_fucking_oracle_mobile.png",
          alt: "",
          loading: "eager" %>
    </picture>

    <div class="oracle-hero-content">

      <p class="oracle-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / TOOLBOX / ARE YOU THE FUCKING ORACLE?
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>ARE YOU THE</span>
        <span>FUCKING</span>
        <span>ORACLE?</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="oracle-intro">
        STOP TREATING YOUR PREDICTIONS LIKE FACTS.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / THE PREDICTION -->
  <section class="oracle-section oracle-pink">
    <div class="oracle-inner">

      <p class="oracle-number">01 / THE PREDICTION</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT ARE YOU<br>
        SO SURE OF?
      </h2>

      <div class="oracle-copy">

        <p>
          You know exactly what's going to happen.
          How the conversation will go, what someone
          is thinking, why something went wrong,
          and how badly everything will end.
        </p>

        <p>
          Except you don't. You're predicting the
          future and treating your prediction
          like information.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is future tripping. You're sitting
          in the present while mentally living
          through something that hasn't happened.
          You've constructed a story, filled in
          the missing details, and started
          reacting as though it's already real.
        </p>

        <p>
          Maybe you're right. Maybe you're wrong.
          But feeling certain doesn't mean
          you actually know.
        </p>

        <p>
          First, identify the prediction.
          What exactly are you claiming
          is going to happen?
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="oracle-callout">
        <p>
          WRITE IT DOWN: "I'M CONVINCED THAT..."
          NOW ASK YOURSELF: HAS THIS ACTUALLY
          HAPPENED, OR AM I PREDICTING IT?
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / FACTS & ASSUMPTIONS -->
  <section class="oracle-section oracle-black">
    <div class="oracle-inner">

      <p class="oracle-number">02 / FACTS &amp; ASSUMPTIONS</p>

      <h2>
        SHOW YOUR<br>
        FUCKING WORK.
      </h2>

      <div class="oracle-copy">

        <p>
          Someone doesn't answer your message.
          That's a fact. Deciding they're angry,
          ignoring you, or don't want anything
          to do with you is an interpretation.
        </p>

        <p>
          Your brain is good at filling in blanks.
          It takes incomplete information,
          invents an explanation, and presents
          the whole thing as reality.
        </p>

        <p>
          CBT calls this mind-reading when you
          assume you know what someone else is
          thinking, and fortune-telling when
          you predict what will happen without
          sufficient evidence.
        </p>

        <p>
          Separate what actually happened
          from what you've decided it means.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="oracle-grid">

        <div class="oracle-box">
          <h3>WHAT DO YOU KNOW?</h3>

          <p>
            Stick to observable facts.
            What happened? What was said?
            What information can you verify?
          </p>

          <p>
            Example: "I sent a message
            three hours ago and haven't
            received a response."
          </p>

          <p>
            No interpretation.
            No imagined motives.
            Just the facts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="oracle-box">
          <h3>WHAT ARE YOU ASSUMING?</h3>

          <p>
            What explanation have you added?
            Are you assuming someone is angry,
            predicting rejection, or deciding
            how a situation will end?
          </p>

          <p>
            Example: "They haven't answered
            because they're pissed off
            and don't want to talk to me."
          </p>

          <p>
            That might be possible.
            It isn't established.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / THE EVIDENCE -->
  <section class="oracle-section oracle-yellow">
    <div class="oracle-inner">

      <p class="oracle-number">03 / THE EVIDENCE</p>

      <h2>
        DOES THE<br>
        EVIDENCE<br>
        HOLD UP?
      </h2>

      <div class="oracle-copy">

        <p>
          Your brain can construct a convincing
          story around almost anything.
          But a convincing story isn't
          necessarily an accurate one.
        </p>

        <p>
          Catastrophizing takes one possible
          problem and turns it into an entire
          disaster. One mistake becomes losing
          your job. Losing your job becomes
          losing your home. Suddenly your
          entire future has collapsed,
          and nothing has actually happened.
        </p>

        <p>
          Examine the evidence before
          accepting the conclusion.
          You're not trying to force
          a positive outcome. You're
          trying to think accurately.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="oracle-grid">

        <div class="oracle-box">
          <h3>WHAT SUPPORTS THE THOUGHT?</h3>

          <p>
            What concrete information
            makes your prediction plausible?
            Has someone explicitly told
            you something? Is there a
            relevant pattern or previous
            experience?
          </p>

          <p>
            Be specific. "I just know"
            and "I have a bad feeling"
            aren't evidence by themselves.
          </p>

          <p>
            A genuine concern deserves
            attention. It still needs
            to be evaluated.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="oracle-box">
          <h3>WHAT DOESN'T FIT?</h3>

          <p>
            What information contradicts
            your prediction? What have
            you overlooked? Are there
            details you don't know?
          </p>

          <p>
            Have you made similar predictions
            that turned out to be wrong?
            Are you ignoring ordinary
            explanations because the
            worst one feels more convincing?
          </p>

          <p>
            Don't only collect evidence
            that agrees with you.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="oracle-callout">
        <p>
          IF SOMEONE ELSE LOOKED AT THE SAME
          EVIDENCE, WOULD THEY REACH THE
          SAME CONCLUSION? WHY OR WHY NOT?
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / OTHER POSSIBILITIES -->
  <section class="oracle-section oracle-purple">
    <div class="oracle-inner">

      <p class="oracle-number">04 / OTHER POSSIBILITIES</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT ELSE<br>
        COULD BE TRUE?
      </h2>

      <div class="oracle-copy">

        <p>
          Your prediction is one possible
          explanation. It isn't necessarily
          the only one.
        </p>

        <p>
          During depression, you may assume
          everything will go badly and
          nothing will improve. During mania,
          you may become excessively certain
          that your ideas are brilliant,
          your decisions are correct,
          and everything will work out.
        </p>

        <p>
          Both can involve confusing how
          certain you feel with how much
          you actually know.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't need to convince yourself
          that everything will be fine.
          You need to recognize that
          more than one outcome is possible.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="oracle-grid">

        <div class="oracle-box">
          <h3>ANOTHER EXPLANATION.</h3>

          <p>
            Come up with at least two
            other reasonable explanations
            for the situation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maybe someone is busy rather
            than angry. Maybe a setback
            is temporary rather than
            permanent. Maybe your exciting
            idea needs more consideration
            before you act.
          </p>

          <p>
            Possibilities aren't promises.
            You're expanding the picture,
            not replacing one prediction
            with another.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="oracle-box">
          <h3>WHAT YOU DON'T KNOW YET.</h3>

          <p>
            Identify the missing information.
            Is there something you could
            ask, verify, or reasonably
            wait to find out?
          </p>

          <p>
            Some questions don't have
            answers yet. You cannot
            manufacture certainty
            through overthinking.
          </p>

          <p>
            "I don't know" is a
            perfectly legitimate answer.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / ANOTHER PERSPECTIVE -->
  <section class="oracle-section oracle-black">
    <div class="oracle-inner">

      <p class="oracle-number">05 / ANOTHER PERSPECTIVE</p>

      <h2>
        GET OUTSIDE<br>
        YOUR OWN HEAD.
      </h2>

      <div class="oracle-copy">

        <p>
          Sometimes you're so invested
          in a prediction that you stop
          questioning it. You've rehearsed
          the outcome so many times
          that it feels inevitable.
        </p>

        <p>
          Ask someone you trust to help
          you separate what happened
          from what you're assuming.
          You aren't asking them to
          promise everything will work out.
          You're asking for another
          perspective on the evidence.
        </p>

        <p>
          Pay attention to your mood.
          Depression can make the future
          look hopeless. Mania can make
          your conclusions feel unusually
          brilliant or unquestionable.
        </p>

       

        <p>
          You can also practice simply
          noticing the prediction:
          "I'm having the thought that..."
          This creates a little space
          between the thought and
          your reaction.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="oracle-callout">
        <p>
          "I'M REALLY CONVINCED SOMETHING
          IS GOING TO HAPPEN, BUT I'M NOT
          SURE I'M LOOKING AT IT CLEARLY.
          CAN I RUN THE FACTS BY YOU?"
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / YOUR NEXT MOVE -->
  <section class="oracle-section oracle-pink">
    <div class="oracle-inner">

      <p class="oracle-number">06 / YOUR NEXT MOVE</p>

      <h2>
        WORK WITH<br>
        WHAT YOU KNOW.
      </h2>

      <div class="oracle-copy">

        <p>
          You've identified the prediction,
          separated facts from assumptions,
          examined the evidence, and
          considered other possibilities.
          Now decide whether anything
          actually needs to happen.
        </p>

        <p>
          If there's a real problem,
          take a reasonable next step.
          Ask a question, gather information,
          make a practical plan, or
          establish a boundary.
        </p>

        <p>
          If there's nothing useful
          to do yet, stop trying
          to solve the future.
          You can tolerate uncertainty
          without filling it with
          imagined disasters.
        </p>

        <p>
          Don't send an angry message,
          make an impulsive purchase,
          cancel something important,
          or reach for alcohol because
          of an outcome you've predicted.
        </p>

        <p>
          Remember the difference:
          planning prepares you for
          a possibility. Future tripping
          makes you suffer through
          an imagined outcome
          before it even happens.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            YOU'RE NOT THE FUCKING ORACLE.
            YOU DON'T KNOW THE FUTURE.
            WORK WITH WHAT'S ACTUALLY
            HAPPENING RIGHT NOW.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="oracle-callout">
        <p>
          WHAT DO YOU ACTUALLY KNOW?
          WHAT CAN YOU CONTROL?
          WHAT IS ONE REASONABLE ACTION
          YOU CAN TAKE RIGHT NOW?
          AND WHAT CAN YOU LEAVE ALONE?
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

</main>
